{"name":"Mourning Blue","hex":"#1651bd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mourning","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of expressing or feeling sorrow or regret; lamentation.","Specifically, the act of expressing or feeling grief regarding a death or loss."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of mourn"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mourning"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mourning","blocks":[{"text":"Mourning is the emotional expression in response to a major life event causing grief, especially loss. It typically occurs as a result of someone's death, especially a loved one."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Girl_in_mourning_dress_holding_framed_photograph_of_her_father.jpg/330px-Girl_in_mourning_dress_holding_framed_photograph_of_her_father.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mourning","wikidata":"Q750652"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Pedro_II_of_Brazil_and_his_sisters.jpg/500px-Pedro_II_of_Brazil_and_his_sisters.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Pedro_II_of_Brazil_and_his_sisters.jpg/1280px-Pedro_II_of_Brazil_and_his_sisters.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Pedro_II_of_Brazil_and_his_sisters.jpg/500px-Pedro_II_of_Brazil_and_his_sisters.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In a painting by Félix Taunay, Emperor Pedro II of Brazil and his sisters, Princesses Francisca and Januária, are depicted wearing mourning clothes due to the death of their father Pedro I in 1834.[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APedro_II_of_Brazil_and_his_sisters.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/K%C3%BCbler-Ross_stages_of_grief_%28in_English%29.png/330px-K%C3%BCbler-Ross_stages_of_grief_%28in_English%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/K%C3%BCbler-Ross_stages_of_grief_%28in_English%29.png/330px-K%C3%BCbler-Ross_stages_of_grief_%28in_English%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kübler-Ross five stages of grief: denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AK%C3%